{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 922 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b7bfc79c2202db437d9323e7e680ecd15845a11d489b0f28e6ec46a58d1ce71a" :edb/db-before-t 921 :edb/db-after-t 922 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534234 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:03:33.569Z" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415272 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415275 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172054 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415281 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415278 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415279 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172097 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415280 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415273 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415274 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415276 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172098 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415277 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172180 1050 780.0 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168663 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415268 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172300 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = ⟨e^{x + y}, e^{x + y}⟩$ and the points $O(0, 0)$ and $A(1, \\ln 2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $F$ is conservative.\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 2e - 1$, where $C_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $OA^(―)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr = 2e - 1$, where $C_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y = \\ln (x + 1)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$." 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172300 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172300 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168675 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415267 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172302 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y) = 4x - 3y$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in $R^{2}$." 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172302 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172302 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(1,-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(4(1)-3(-3))-(4(0)-3(2)) \\\\  & =4+9+6 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171675 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415263 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172675 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y, z) = xyz + y^{2}$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1, 0,-1)$ to $(2, 1, 1)$ in $R^{3}$." 13194139534234 17592186045417 false] [17592186172675 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186172675 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,-1)$ and $B(2,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,1)-f(1,0,-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+{1}^{2})-(1\\cdot 0\\cdot (-1)+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415194 1080 "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415194 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415194 1083 #uuid "01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415195 1080 "I only" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415195 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415195 1083 #uuid "036e6f03-ee36-5c5f-b814-7fbdad1c16c8" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17