orem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0)$ and $B(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)}-1{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415262 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1069 "q-125742" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1072 #uuid "a75809b4-9c5b-5b68-8717-3a8614d8377f" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415278 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,3,1)$ and $B(0,1,-1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,1,-1)-f(-2,3,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{0+1}-(1){e}^{-2+3} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415259 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1069 "q-125741" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1072 #uuid "cd1e238f-7dc4-513f-b157-39e9312d6861" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415279 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,1)$ and $B(2,-2,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,-2,0)-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-(-2))-(1\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-0) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415260 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1069 "q-125733" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1072 #uuid "d068dd74-b0d6-5e4a-8ca9-75d15ab6e3ed" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415280 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,2)$ and $B(\\pi ,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(\\pi ,1)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(2){e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-2e \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2{e}^{2}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415266 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1069 "q-125736" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1072 #uuid "ef46b0f0-6f4c-53dd-b0f1-5e1b301fe037" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true] [17592186415281 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534234 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" 17592186415194 "answer-036e6f03-ee36-5c5f-b814-7fbdad1c16c8" 17592186415195 "answer-05ed4975-6d31-5ec6-a093-5c910fbba055" 17592186415196 "answer-09235254-4d47-5c19-a8cc-f2c8d86165e2" 17592186415197 "answer-0b5add66-bcf6-5621-80cf-899ef43c417d" 17592186415198 "answer-14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" 17592186415199 "answer-15a9cbc7-f277-56ad-a34d-b1e67b9ccb79" 17592186415200 "answer-17551007-968c-50bb-8713-508a56887419" 17592186415201 "answer-1f497857-d2ea-515b-ac70-f83db9b8d663" 17592186415202 "answer-21edad9a-788d-531c-a881-1f0e31295a9f" 17592186415203 "answer-23fa8d02-75d6-5d90-ad84-bf573bb9d9df" 17592186415204 "answer-260ed446-43fe-5beb-ab7d-78b6125c1cd3" 17592186415205 "answer-2723dc72-fd63-54a4-bb39-b1f78ca7b53b" 17592186415206 "answer-2d8bc88c-65ec-5ed0-861b-da4a28dd2e6c" 17592186415207 "answer-32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" 17592186415208 "answer-394650d1-8e19-51ba-a71f-b0fc64e540e7" 17592186415209 "answer-3e05852b-fb2c-53ef-8b30-791b2b7932ac" 17592186415210 "answer-4199a3f5-5c75-59ad-ae31-6dbe20749db3" 17592186415211 "answer-43de946b-6276-5f5e-981d-3f9e49ef0b6b" 17592186415212 "answer-450c00f1-3112-5f7e-b618-7f3d64cf06cf" 17592186415213 "answer-485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" 17592186415214 "answer-50955a5e-a870-5833-935b-0d75056ca130" 17592186415215 "answer-52fd34b1-d7f9-5a57-9656-43e29d60efa5" 17592186415216 "answer-5c258c27-caa9-5d08-8078-126afe0392e1" 17592186415217 "answer-5d32c371-2553-5a69-82d8-90b81f6734fa" 17592186415218 "answer-61d2add2-3ad5-5382-bacd-16dbd27b4700" 1759